text = "PRIVACY POLICY

Last Updated: The moment somebody remembered that we were supposed to have a Privacy Policy.

Welcome to the Privacy Policy for this Software.

This document explains what information may be collected, what information may not be collected, what information we wish we could collect but absolutely cannot, what information your computer thinks we collected, and what the Captain claims he has never seen.

PLEASE READ THIS PRIVACY POLICY CAREFULLY.

By installing, launching, opening, closing, minimizing, maximizing, clicking, double-clicking, right-clicking, accidentally clicking, intentionally clicking, or merely looking at this Software, you acknowledge that you have encountered this Privacy Policy.

You do not necessarily need to understand it.

We barely do.

------------------------------------------------------------

1. WHO ARE WE?

We are the Developers.

We created this Software.

We are not the government.

We are not your internet provider.

We are not your bank.

We are not your parents.

We are not your school.

We are not your teacher.

We are not your Wi-Fi router.

We are definitely not your webcam.

We are simply the people who made this questionable piece of software and decided that it probably needed a Privacy Policy.

For the purposes of this Privacy Policy, we may refer to ourselves as:

- \"We\"
- \"Us\"
- \"Our\"
- \"The Developers\"
- \"The Company\"
- \"The Crew\"
- \"The Pirates\"
- \"The People Who Made This\"
- \"The Captain\"
- \"Those Guys\"
- \"Whoever Is Maintaining The Project\"

All of these terms mean approximately the same thing.

------------------------------------------------------------

2. WHAT IS THIS PRIVACY POLICY?

This Privacy Policy explains how information may be handled when you use the Software.

It is intended to provide transparency.

It is also intended to look extremely official.

Please do not let the extremely official formatting convince you that we have an office.

We do not necessarily have an office.

We may have desks.

We may have chairs.

One of us may have a very suspiciously large collection of cables.

That does not count as an office.

------------------------------------------------------------

3. OUR APPROACH TO PRIVACY

We believe privacy is important.

Very important.

Extremely important.

So important that we wrote this enormous document explaining how much we care about it.

We believe users should understand what information software collects.

We also believe users should not have to read 47 pages of legal text just to launch an emulator.

Unfortunately, we have already written 47 pages of legal text.

We apologize.

------------------------------------------------------------

4. INFORMATION WE COLLECT

The Software may collect certain information depending on how it is configured and what features you use.

However, we do not automatically collect every piece of information that exists on your computer.

We do not have magical access to everything.

We cannot simply look at your hard drive and say:

\"Ah yes. Folder named 'homework'. Very suspicious.\"

We cannot see your entire computer simply because you installed the Software.

Your files remain your files.

Your folders remain your folders.

Your extremely embarrassing Downloads folder remains your responsibility.

------------------------------------------------------------

5. INFORMATION WE MAY COLLECT

Depending on the version of the Software, we may collect limited technical information such as:

- Software version.
- Operating system.
- General hardware information.
- Basic performance information.
- Error messages.
- Crash information.
- Feature usage statistics.
- Installation status.
- Update status.
- General diagnostic information.

This information may help us determine why the Software suddenly decided to stop working at 2:14 PM on a Tuesday.

We may use this information to improve the Software.

We may also stare at a crash log for 45 minutes and eventually say:

\"That's weird.\"

------------------------------------------------------------

6. INFORMATION WE DO NOT WANT

There are many types of information we do not need.

For example:

- Your password.
- Your bank account number.
- Your credit card number.
- Your school password.
- Your social media password.
- Your email password.
- Your Wi-Fi password.
- Your PIN.
- Your secret Minecraft world seed.
- Your diary.
- Your browser history.
- Your search history.
- Your embarrassing screenshots.
- Your extremely questionable folder called \"stuff\".

Please do not send us these things.

Especially the passwords.

We do not want them.

------------------------------------------------------------

7. PASSWORDS

WE DO NOT WANT YOUR PASSWORD.

If someone claiming to be us asks for your password, do not give it to them.

If an email claiming to be from us says:

\"URGENT! SEND PASSWORD OR CAPTAIN WILL BE ANGRY!\"

do not send your password.

If a popup inside the Software says:

\"ENTER YOUR PASSWORD TO UNLOCK THE TREASURE\"

do not enter your password.

There is no treasure.

Probably.

If somebody asks for your password and offers you a pirate hat in exchange:

Still no.

------------------------------------------------------------

8. YOUR FILES

The Software may need access to files that you explicitly choose to open or use.

For example, if you select a file for the Software to process, the Software obviously needs to access that file.

This does not mean we automatically receive a copy of it.

We are not secretly downloading your files onto a pirate ship.

We do not have a pirate ship.

We have established this already.

------------------------------------------------------------

9. YOUR GAME FILES

If you use the Software with game files, you are responsible for ensuring that you have the appropriate rights to use those files.

We do not automatically know where your files came from.

We do not sit in a control room monitoring your Downloads folder.

We are not sitting there saying:

\"Captain! Another file has appeared!\"

\"Excellent! Add it to the spreadsheet!\"

No such spreadsheet exists.

------------------------------------------------------------

10. YOUR BROWSER HISTORY

We do not need your browser history.

We do not want your browser history.

We especially do not want to know what you searched at 3:00 AM last Friday.

If your browser history is embarrassing, that is between you and your browser.

Please leave us out of it.

------------------------------------------------------------

11. SEARCH QUERIES

If the Software includes a search feature, information you enter into that feature may be processed by the Software in order to provide the requested functionality.

Depending on how the feature is implemented, search queries may be processed locally.

If a search query is sent to an external service, that service may have its own privacy policy.

Please review the privacy policy of third-party services where appropriate.

Also, please stop searching for:

\"how to become a pirate legally\"

We have no idea.

------------------------------------------------------------

12. COOKIES

The Software may use cookies where technically necessary.

Cookies are small pieces of information used by software and websites.

They are not chocolate chip cookies.

They cannot be eaten.

Do not attempt to eat a cookie from your computer.

If you somehow find an actual cookie inside your computer, remove it immediately.

Do not put milk inside your computer.

We cannot believe we have to say this.

------------------------------------------------------------

13. TRACKING

We do not intend to track your physical location through supernatural pirate magic.

We do not have a crystal ball.

We do not have a GPS satellite.

We do not have a guy sitting in a room drawing dots on a map.

We may receive general technical information when you interact with online services, but this does not mean we automatically know where you live.

We do not need to know where you live.

Please remain where you are.

------------------------------------------------------------

14. IP ADDRESSES

If the Software connects to an online service, that service may technically receive your IP address.

This is normal internet behavior.

An IP address may provide general information about a network connection.

An IP address is not necessarily your exact physical address.

We are not going to show up at your house because your IP address said you were somewhere nearby.

We don't even know where the Captain lives.

------------------------------------------------------------

15. LOCATION INFORMATION

The Software does not require precise GPS location information for its basic functionality.

We do not need to know whether you are:

- In your bedroom.
- In your kitchen.
- At school.
- At a friend's house.
- In a library.
- On a bus.
- On a boat.
- In a submarine.

Especially the submarine.

We are still confused about that.

------------------------------------------------------------

16. CAMERA ACCESS

The Software does not require access to your camera for normal operation unless explicitly stated by a particular feature.

If a feature does not need your camera, there is no reason for it to access your camera.

If you ever see a suspicious application asking for camera access without a clear reason:

Do not blindly approve it.

Think before clicking.

Your webcam is not decorative.

------------------------------------------------------------

17. MICROPHONE ACCESS

The same principle applies to your microphone.

If the Software does not require your microphone, it should not need access to it.

We are not interested in listening to:

- Your conversations.
- Your music.
- Your pets.
- Your keyboard.
- Your parents asking whether you've done your homework.
- You screaming at the computer because an emulator crashed.

Although the last one might be funny.

We still don't need the recording.

------------------------------------------------------------

18. CONTACTS

The Software does not need your contact list for normal operation.

We do not need to know who your friends are.

We do not need to know who your family members are.

We do not need to know who you keep texting at midnight.

Your contacts are yours.

Keep them.

------------------------------------------------------------

19. PERSONAL INFORMATION

Personal information may include information that can identify or reasonably relate to an individual.

Examples may include:

- Name.
- Email address.
- Account information.
- Device information.
- IP address.
- Other information depending on context.

We encourage you not to provide unnecessary personal information when contacting us.

If you send us an email saying:

\"HELP THE PROGRAM DOESN'T WORK\"

that is enough.

You do not need to include:

\"My name is [full legal name], I live at [full address], my password is [password], and my mother's maiden name is [name].\"

Please don't.

------------------------------------------------------------

20. EMAIL

If you contact us through email, we will receive the information you choose to include in your message.

This may include your email address, name, message, attachments, and other information you voluntarily provide.

Please remember:

The internet is not a magical privacy vacuum.

Do not send sensitive information unless necessary.

If your message contains a password, delete the password and send the message again.

We are serious.

------------------------------------------------------------

21. SUPPORT REQUESTS

When you contact support, we may ask for technical information needed to understand your problem.

For example:

\"What operating system are you using?\"

is reasonable.

\"Please send us your bank password.\"

is not.

\"Please send us a screenshot of the error.\"

is reasonable.

\"Please send us a screenshot of your entire desktop including all your private conversations.\"

is probably unnecessary.

Use common sense.

------------------------------------------------------------

22. CRASH REPORTS

If crash reporting is enabled, technical information about a crash may be collected.

Crash information can help us determine what caused the Software to stop functioning.

It may include:

- Error messages.
- Stack traces.
- Software version.
- Operating system.
- Hardware information.
- Relevant configuration information.

It should not require us to know your deepest secrets.

Your computer crashing does not mean your privacy has crashed.

------------------------------------------------------------

23. LOG FILES

The Software may create local log files.

Logs may contain technical information used to diagnose problems.

Logs might contain things like:

\"Loading emulator.\"

\"Loading configuration.\"

\"Something went wrong.\"

\"Something went very wrong.\"

\"WHY.\"

\"Attempting recovery.\"

\"Recovery unsuccessful.\"

\"Developer has been notified.\"

\"Developer has not actually been notified.\"

We are working on that last one.

------------------------------------------------------------

24. LOCAL STORAGE

Some settings may be stored locally on your device.

These may include:

- Preferences.
- Configuration settings.
- Save information.
- Recently used options.
- Interface settings.

Local storage means the information stays on your device unless another process copies or transmits it.

Please remember that other applications on your computer may also have access to files depending on your operating system permissions.

We cannot control every application installed on your computer.

------------------------------------------------------------

25. DATA SECURITY

We take reasonable measures to protect information that we control.

However, no computer system is perfectly secure.

No network is perfectly secure.

No software is perfectly secure.

No password is perfectly secure.

\"Password123\" is especially not secure.

Neither is:

\"qwerty\"

Neither is:

\"admin\"

Neither is:

\"pirate\"

We checked.

Please use strong passwords.

------------------------------------------------------------

26. DATA BREACHES

If a security incident occurs that affects information under our control, we may take appropriate steps to investigate and respond.

Depending on applicable law, we may also notify affected users or authorities where required.

We hope this never happens.

If it does happen, please remain calm.

Do not immediately assume that every piece of information on your computer has been stolen.

------------------------------------------------------------

27. DATA RETENTION

We retain information only for as long as reasonably necessary for the purposes for which it was collected, unless a longer retention period is required or permitted by law.

Technical logs may eventually be deleted.

Support emails may remain available for record-keeping purposes.

Crash reports may be retained for debugging.

Your embarrassing search for \"pirate hats near me\" is not something we intend to archive forever.

------------------------------------------------------------

28. DELETION

Depending on the type of information and applicable law, you may have rights regarding deletion of personal information.

If you want information removed, contact the appropriate support channel.

Please understand that deleting information from every possible backup instantly may not always be technically possible.

Computers have backups.

Backups have backups.

Somewhere, there is probably a backup of a backup.

We are sorry.

------------------------------------------------------------

29. BACKUPS

We may maintain backups for security, reliability, or operational purposes.

Backups are not necessarily immediately deleted when information is deleted from an active system.

This is normal.

We are not secretly keeping your information in a treasure chest.

If we did have a treasure chest, we would probably put gold in it.

Not your email address.

------------------------------------------------------------

30. THIRD-PARTY SERVICES

The Software may interact with third-party services.

These services may have their own privacy policies.

We are not responsible for the privacy practices of third parties that we do not control.

If you click a link and leave our Software, you should review the privacy policy of the destination service.

Do not assume every website on the internet follows the same rules.

Some websites are weird.

Some websites are extremely weird.

------------------------------------------------------------

31. ADVERTISING

Advertisements may be provided by third-party services.

Third-party advertising providers may collect information according to their own policies.

We may not control what information those services collect.

Advertisements may use technical information to determine which advertisements to display.

This does not mean someone is personally sitting behind a computer saying:

\"Show this person an advertisement for socks.\"

Although somebody probably is.

------------------------------------------------------------

32. THE ADVERTISEMENTS ARE NOT OUR FAULT

If an advertisement says:

\"YOU WON $1,000,000!\"

you probably did not.

If an advertisement says:

\"YOUR COMPUTER HAS 47 VIRUSES!\"

you should probably verify that claim.

If an advertisement says:

\"CLICK HERE TO BECOME A PIRATE!\"

we cannot recommend it.

We are already pirates enough.

------------------------------------------------------------

33. POPUPS

Certain fictional or game-related versions of the Software may display simulated advertisements or popup windows.

These may appear intentionally as part of the Software's experience.

These simulated advertisements are not necessarily real advertisements.

If a popup says:

\"CONGRATULATIONS! YOU ARE THE 1,000,000TH VISITOR!\"

you are probably not.

If a popup says:

\"ERROR: NOT ENOUGH RAM\"

your RAM may actually be completely fine.

The Software may simply be dramatic.

------------------------------------------------------------

34. ANALYTICS

If analytics are enabled, analytics may help us understand general usage patterns.

For example:

- Which features are used.
- Which features crash.
- Which screens are opened.
- Which buttons are pressed.
- How frequently certain features are used.

Analytics should be designed to provide useful information without unnecessarily identifying individual users.

We don't need to know that Steve from Apartment 4B clicked the Settings button fourteen times.

Unless Steve is doing something suspicious.

------------------------------------------------------------

35. CHILDREN'S PRIVACY

The Software is not designed to intentionally collect unnecessary personal information from children.

We do not intentionally ask children to provide sensitive information.

If you are a young user, do not provide personal information simply because an application asks for it.

If a software program asks you for your home address, school, phone number, and favorite pizza before letting you open a settings menu:

Close it.

Immediately.

------------------------------------------------------------

36. PARENTS AND GUARDIANS

Parents and guardians are encouraged to help younger users understand online privacy.

Do not assume that because an application has a cute logo it is trustworthy.

Do not assume that because a website has a padlock it is automatically safe.

Do not assume that because a person on the internet calls themselves \"CaptainPirateOfficial\" they are actually the Captain.

They probably aren't.

------------------------------------------------------------

37. SOCIAL MEDIA

We may maintain social media accounts for the Software or development project.

Interactions on third-party social media platforms are subject to the privacy policies of those platforms.

If you publicly comment on one of our posts, other people may see your comment.

This is generally how public comments work.

Please do not publicly post your password.

Seriously.

------------------------------------------------------------

38. USER CONTENT

If you voluntarily submit content to us, such as:

- Bug reports.
- Suggestions.
- Screenshots.
- Feedback.
- Messages.
- Fan art.
- Memes.
- Pirate drawings.
- Extremely long explanations of why the emulator crashed.

we may receive and process that content for purposes such as support, development, moderation, or communication.

If you send us a meme, we may laugh.

We cannot guarantee that we will laugh.

Humor is subjective.

------------------------------------------------------------

39. MEMES

Memes submitted to us may be viewed by the development team.

If the meme is terrible, we reserve the right to silently judge it.

This is not a legal right.

It is an emotional right.

------------------------------------------------------------

40. DATA SOLD TO PIRATES

We do not sell your personal information to pirates.

Mainly because pirates do not have a functional data marketplace.

Also because we do not want to sell your personal information.

If somebody offers to buy your personal information in exchange for gold coins, do not accept.

Unless the gold coins are real.

Then contact us.

Just kidding.

Do not.

------------------------------------------------------------

41. YOUR DATA IS NOT TREASURE

Your information is not treasure.

We do not bury it.

We do not put it in chests.

We do not draw maps leading to it.

We do not mark it with a giant red X.

We do not guard it with skeletons.

We do not make you walk across an island to retrieve it.

Your information should be handled through boring computer systems.

This is preferable.

------------------------------------------------------------

42. INTERNATIONAL DATA TRANSFERS

Depending on where you live and where our services operate, information may be processed in countries other than your own.

Different countries may have different privacy laws.

Where required, appropriate safeguards may be used for international transfers.

We realize this sounds extremely boring.

That is because it is.

Privacy law is sometimes boring.

Pirate law is significantly more entertaining.

Unfortunately, pirate law is not real.

------------------------------------------------------------

43. YOUR PRIVACY RIGHTS

Depending on your location, you may have rights concerning your personal information.

These may include rights to:

- Access certain information.
- Correct inaccurate information.
- Request deletion.
- Object to certain processing.
- Restrict certain processing.
- Request a copy of information.
- Withdraw consent where processing is based on consent.

The exact rights available to you depend on applicable law.

We cannot provide legal advice through this Privacy Policy.

The parrot cannot provide legal advice either.

------------------------------------------------------------

44. ACCESS REQUESTS

If you request access to personal information associated with you, we may need enough information to verify that you are actually the person making the request.

We do this to protect privacy.

Otherwise, anyone could email:

\"Hello, please give me all of Steve's information.\"

and we would have to say:

\"No.\"

Steve's information belongs to Steve.

Probably.

We don't even know who Steve is.

------------------------------------------------------------

45. CORRECTION REQUESTS

If information we hold about you is inaccurate, you may be able to request correction.

For example:

If your name is \"Santiago\" and we somehow have it recorded as \"Captain Banana\":

we should probably fix that.

Unless your name actually is Captain Banana.

In which case:

Congratulations.

------------------------------------------------------------

46. DELETION REQUESTS

If applicable law gives you the right to request deletion of certain information, you may contact us through the appropriate channel.

We will review the request.

Some information may need to be retained where required by law or for legitimate technical or security purposes.

No, this does not mean we are hiding a secret vault.

Probably.

------------------------------------------------------------

47. CONSENT

Where required, we may ask for consent before collecting or using certain information.

Consent should be voluntary.

If you don't understand what you're agreeing to, you should take the time to understand it.

This Privacy Policy is approximately 900 miles long, so we understand if you skipped some of it.

------------------------------------------------------------

48. WITHDRAWING CONSENT

Where processing is based on consent, you may generally withdraw that consent.

Withdrawing consent may affect certain features.

If you disable something and the Software stops doing that thing, this is probably intentional.

If you disable everything and the Software becomes a blank white screen:

We have bigger problems.

------------------------------------------------------------

49. DO NOT TRACK

Some browsers and systems provide \"Do Not Track\" signals.

Support for such signals may vary depending on the service.

We may not respond to every possible browser privacy setting.

Technology is complicated.

There are approximately seven million privacy settings on the internet.

We cannot keep track of all of them.

------------------------------------------------------------

50. SECURITY RECOMMENDATIONS

We recommend that you:

- Keep your operating system updated.
- Keep the Software updated.
- Use strong passwords.
- Use different passwords for different accounts.
- Enable multi-factor authentication where available.
- Avoid suspicious links.
- Avoid suspicious downloads.
- Avoid suspicious USB drives.
- Avoid suspicious pirates.

The final recommendation is surprisingly difficult.

------------------------------------------------------------

51. USB DRIVES

Do not plug random USB drives into your computer.

If someone hands you a USB drive and says:

\"Don't worry, it's totally safe.\"

you should worry.

Especially if it is labeled:

\"FREE MONEY\"

or:

\"IMPORTANT SECRET\"

or:

\"DO NOT OPEN\"

Actually, especially the last one.

------------------------------------------------------------

52. PHISHING

Phishing is when someone attempts to trick you into providing sensitive information.

Common examples include messages pretending to be:

- Banks.
- Schools.
- Game companies.
- Friends.
- Employers.
- Governments.
- Pirates.

If someone says:

\"ARRR, GIVE ME YOUR PASSWORD OR I WILL WALK THE PLANK!\"

do not give them your password.

We have repeatedly discussed this.

------------------------------------------------------------

53. IMPERSONATION

We will not intentionally impersonate another company, developer, government, or individual.

If somebody contacts you claiming to be us, verify that the communication is genuine.

If they ask for money, passwords, or extremely suspicious information:

Stop.

Think.

Verify.

Then probably don't do it.

------------------------------------------------------------

54. DATA MINIMIZATION

We believe in collecting only information that is reasonably necessary for a particular purpose.

We do not need:

Your blood type.

Your shoe size.

Your favorite dinosaur.

Your Minecraft username.

Your lunch order.

Your zodiac sign.

Your first pet's name.

Your secret handshake.

Your pirate rank.

Unless the Software somehow becomes a pirate dating application.

It has not.

Please stop asking.

------------------------------------------------------------

55. INFORMATION YOU VOLUNTARILY PROVIDE

If you voluntarily provide information, you are responsible for deciding whether that information is appropriate to share.

Do not send us:

- Passwords.
- Private keys.
- Recovery codes.
- Bank details.
- Government identification numbers.
- Sensitive medical information.
- Private photographs.

Unless specifically required by a legitimate process.

And even then, verify who you are sending it to.

------------------------------------------------------------

56. PRIVATE KEYS

Never send your private cryptographic keys to strangers.

Never send them to us.

Never send them to the Captain.

Never send them to the parrot.

The parrot doesn't even know what cryptography is.

------------------------------------------------------------

57. YOUR DEVICE

We may receive limited technical information about your device when you interact with online services.

This may include operating system type, software version, or other technical information.

We do not automatically receive a magical list of every file on your device.

Computers do not work like that.

Usually.

------------------------------------------------------------

58. THE DOWNLOADS FOLDER

We solemnly promise not to open your Downloads folder just because it exists.

We don't know why there are 14 copies of:

setup_final.exe

setup_final2.exe

setup_final_REAL.exe

setup_final_REAL2.exe

setup_final_USE_THIS_ONE.exe

Please clean your Downloads folder.

------------------------------------------------------------

59. DESKTOP PRIVACY

Your desktop is your desktop.

We do not need to know what is on it.

If your desktop contains 347 icons, that is your business.

If your desktop contains one folder named:

\"ORGANIZE LATER\"

we already know that folder will never be organized.

------------------------------------------------------------

60. SCREEN CAPTURE

The Software should not capture your screen unless a feature explicitly requires screen capture and you have provided the appropriate permission.

If you see an application unexpectedly requesting screen capture access, consider whether the request makes sense.

If it doesn't:

Don't allow it.

Your screen is yours.

------------------------------------------------------------

61. KEYBOARD INPUT

The Software processes keyboard input when necessary for normal functionality.

For example, if you type something into a text field, the Software needs to know what you typed.

This does not mean the Software should record every key you press everywhere on your computer.

We do not need to know every time you type the letter \"A\".

There are many A's.

We are not interested.

------------------------------------------------------------

62. CLIPBOARD

If the Software provides copy-and-paste functionality, it may interact with clipboard contents when you explicitly use that functionality.

Do not assume every application needs access to your clipboard.

If an application asks for clipboard access without a clear reason, think carefully before allowing it.

Your clipboard may contain sensitive information.

It may also contain the word:

banana

We cannot know.

------------------------------------------------------------

63. NOTIFICATIONS

If notifications are enabled, the Software may display notifications related to its functionality.

Notifications may include:

- Updates.
- Downloads.
- Errors.
- Warnings.
- Important messages.
- Completely unnecessary pirate jokes.

You may disable notifications where supported.

The Captain will be disappointed.

------------------------------------------------------------

64. PRIVACY SETTINGS

If the Software provides privacy settings, you may use them to control available features.

We recommend reviewing privacy settings occasionally.

Software changes.

Operating systems change.

The internet changes.

The Captain changes his mind approximately every six minutes.

------------------------------------------------------------

65. CHANGES TO THIS PRIVACY POLICY

We may update this Privacy Policy from time to time.

When we make changes, the updated date may be changed.

If the changes are significant, additional notice may be provided where appropriate.

We may not personally send you a carrier pigeon.

We do not own a carrier pigeon.

We have the parrot.

The parrot is not qualified to deliver legal notices.

------------------------------------------------------------

66. THE PARROT'S PRIVACY

The parrot has requested that we include this section.

The parrot's personal information includes:

- Name: Parrot.
- Age: Unknown.
- Location: Probably somewhere.
- Occupation: Parrot.
- Favorite food: Seeds.
- Favorite activity: Screaming.
- Password: Unknown.
- Email: Unknown.
- IP address: Probably not applicable.

The Developers respect the parrot's privacy.

The parrot has declined to comment.

------------------------------------------------------------

67. THE CAPTAIN'S PRIVACY

The Captain has also requested privacy.

The Captain's personal information includes:

Name: Captain.

Address: Unknown.

Age: Classified.

Favorite food: Sandwiches.

Favorite operating system: Depends on the day.

Password: We are not writing it here.

Pet: The parrot.

Current location: Probably not the submarine.

The Captain's privacy is important.

Please do not attempt to locate the Captain.

------------------------------------------------------------

68. EMPLOYEE PRIVACY

The Developers may have team members who contribute to the Software.

We do not publish private information about team members without appropriate authorization.

Please do not attempt to identify developers based on their typing style.

Please do not send developers strange messages at 2 AM.

Please do not ask developers for their passwords.

Please do not send the developers pictures of your router.

Unless something is seriously wrong with the router.

Then maybe.

------------------------------------------------------------

69. LAW ENFORCEMENT

We may disclose information when required to do so by applicable law or valid legal process.

We do not voluntarily hand over information simply because someone says:

\"Trust me, I'm the police.\"

Verify everything properly.

The internet is full of people claiming to be important.

Some are.

Some are not.

------------------------------------------------------------

70. LEGAL REQUESTS

If we receive a valid legal request for information, we may be required to comply.

We may challenge requests where appropriate.

We may also be unable to provide information that we simply do not possess.

You cannot ask us to provide the location of your missing sock.

We don't have it.

------------------------------------------------------------

71. GOVERNMENT REQUESTS

Government agencies may request information under applicable laws.

We will handle such requests according to the law.

We do not have a secret government hotline.

We do not have a red phone.

We do have a phone.

It is probably on silent.

------------------------------------------------------------

72. DATA OWNERSHIP

Where you provide your own personal information, you generally retain your rights over that information subject to applicable law.

We do not suddenly own your name because you typed it into a form.

If your name is Steve, it remains Steve.

Congratulations, Steve.

------------------------------------------------------------

73. THIRD-PARTY LINKS

The Software may contain links to third-party websites.

We are not responsible for the privacy practices of those websites.

Before providing personal information to a third-party website, review its privacy policy.

If the website's privacy policy begins with:

\"HAHA WE OWN YOUR SOUL\"

you may want to leave.

------------------------------------------------------------

74. SOCIAL ENGINEERING

We will never intentionally ask you to bypass security controls for the purpose of giving us access to private information.

If someone says:

\"Just disable your antivirus for five minutes.\"

Ask why.

If the answer is:

\"Because the Captain said so.\"

That is not a sufficient answer.

------------------------------------------------------------

75. ANTI-VIRUS SOFTWARE

Security software may identify the Software as suspicious depending on how the Software is packaged or distributed.

A warning does not automatically mean the Software is malicious.

However, you should never ignore security warnings blindly.

Investigate the warning.

Verify the source.

Use common sense.

If the warning says:

\"THIS FILE WILL DESTROY EVERYTHING\"

perhaps do not run it immediately.

------------------------------------------------------------

76. OPEN SOURCE COMPONENTS

The Software may contain third-party libraries or open-source components.

Those components may have their own licenses and privacy implications.

Where applicable, information about those components may be included with the Software.

We thank the people who create open-source software.

Without them, many projects would probably explode.

------------------------------------------------------------

77. TELEMETRY

If telemetry is enabled, telemetry may provide anonymous or aggregated information about Software usage.

Telemetry may help answer questions such as:

\"Does this button work?\"

\"How often does the emulator crash?\"

\"Why is everybody clicking this?\"

\"What happened to the settings menu?\"

\"Who removed the settings menu?\"

\"Who is responsible?\"

\"Captain?\"

The Captain has declined to comment.

------------------------------------------------------------

78. ANONYMOUS DATA

Anonymous or aggregated information is information that is not intended to identify individual users.

For example:

\"30% of users clicked this button.\"

is different from:

\"Steve clicked this button at 4:31 PM.\"

We prefer the first one.

Steve prefers the second one.

We don't know why.

------------------------------------------------------------

79. AGGREGATED DATA

Aggregated data may be used to understand general usage patterns.

For example:

- Average session length.
- Most commonly used features.
- Common crash types.
- General operating system distribution.

We may learn that 72% of users are using Windows.

We may learn that 18% are using Linux.

We may learn that 10% are using something we cannot identify.

We will investigate.

------------------------------------------------------------

80. DATA ACCURACY

We attempt to keep information accurate where appropriate.

However, technical systems can contain errors.

If our system thinks you are a pirate named Steve Banana:

Please contact us.

We will investigate.

------------------------------------------------------------

81. DATA STORAGE

Information may be stored electronically.

Electronic storage can fail.

Hard drives can fail.

SSDs can fail.

Servers can fail.

Cloud services can fail.

USB drives can disappear mysteriously.

Backups can fail.

The only thing guaranteed in computing is that something will eventually break.

------------------------------------------------------------

82. OFFLINE USE

Some features may work entirely offline.

When the Software is offline, it cannot communicate with online services that require an internet connection.

This is a very complicated way of saying:

No internet = no internet.

Thank you for attending this technical explanation.

------------------------------------------------------------

83. ONLINE FEATURES

Online features may require network communication.

When online, the Software may communicate with servers necessary to provide the requested feature.

Network communication may expose certain technical information such as IP addresses.

This is normal.

The internet would be significantly less useful if computers could not communicate with other computers.

------------------------------------------------------------

84. SERVER LOGS

Servers may maintain technical logs.

These logs may include information such as:

- IP addresses.
- Request times.
- Error codes.
- Browser or client information.
- Requested resources.

These logs help keep services running.

Without logs, diagnosing certain problems would involve asking the server:

\"Why did you die?\"

and hoping it answers.

Servers are not particularly talkative.

------------------------------------------------------------

85. RETENTION OF SERVER LOGS

Server logs may be retained for limited periods depending on operational and security requirements.

After they are no longer needed, they may be deleted or anonymized.

We are not keeping every server log until the heat death of the universe.

Probably.

------------------------------------------------------------

86. ACCOUNT INFORMATION

If the Software provides optional accounts, account information may be processed to provide account functionality.

You may be required to provide basic information such as an email address.

Do not use someone else's email address without permission.

Do not create 900 accounts just because the registration form allows it.

We will become suspicious.

------------------------------------------------------------

87. ACCOUNT SECURITY

You are responsible for protecting your account credentials.

If someone gains access to your account because you gave them your password, that is a problem.

If someone gains access because you used:

password

as your password:

That is also a problem.

Please use a stronger password.

------------------------------------------------------------

88. TWO-FACTOR AUTHENTICATION

Where available, multi-factor authentication may provide additional security.

We recommend using it.

It makes your account harder to access even if someone obtains your password.

Yes, it takes an extra few seconds.

No, you will not perish.

Please enable it.

------------------------------------------------------------

89. YOUR RESPONSIBILITY

Privacy is a shared responsibility.

We are responsible for how we handle information under our control.

You are responsible for the information you choose to share.

Third parties are responsible for their own services.

The internet is responsible for approximately none of this.

------------------------------------------------------------

90. THE INTERNET

The internet is enormous.

There are billions of devices.

Millions of websites.

Countless applications.

Many strange forums.

Thousands of suspicious download buttons.

At least one website dedicated entirely to pictures of potatoes.

We cannot control the internet.

Nobody can.

Probably not even the Captain.

------------------------------------------------------------

91. SUSPICIOUS WEBSITES

If a website asks for personal information that seems unnecessary, stop and think.

If a website asks you to:

\"ENTER YOUR PASSWORD TO PROVE YOU ARE HUMAN\"

do not.

If a website asks you to:

\"DOWNLOAD THIS FILE TO REMOVE 83 VIRUSES\"

do not.

If a website asks you to:

\"PAY $500 IN GIFT CARDS TO UNLOCK YOUR COMPUTER\"

definitely do not.

Call someone you trust.

------------------------------------------------------------

92. PHISHING EMAILS

Be careful with emails that create urgency.

Examples:

\"ACT NOW!\"

\"YOUR ACCOUNT WILL BE DELETED!\"

\"FINAL WARNING!\"

\"CAPTAIN WILL BE ANGRY!\"

Urgency is commonly used to make people act before thinking.

Slow down.

Check the sender.

Check the link.

Do not panic.

------------------------------------------------------------

93. LINKS

Before clicking a link, consider where it leads.

If the link looks suspicious, do not click it.

If the link says:

free-money.example

and you know perfectly well that free money does not exist:

don't.

The Captain has never received free money.

Except once.

It was a coupon.

It expired.

------------------------------------------------------------

94. PRIVACY INCIDENTS

If you believe your information has been mishandled, contact us through an appropriate support channel.

Provide enough information for us to investigate.

Please do not send us your entire hard drive.

We don't have enough USB ports.

------------------------------------------------------------

95. REPORTING SECURITY PROBLEMS

If you discover a security vulnerability, please report it responsibly.

Do not publicly publish sensitive information before the issue can be addressed where responsible disclosure is appropriate.

Do not exploit the vulnerability just because you can.

Do not steal information to prove that you could steal information.

That is not a good bug report.

------------------------------------------------------------

96. RESPONSIBLE DISCLOSURE

A good security report might include:

- Description of the issue.
- Steps to reproduce it.
- Affected version.
- Expected behavior.
- Actual behavior.
- Potential impact.

A bad security report might say:

\"I HACKED YOU LOL\"

and nothing else.

Please be more specific.

------------------------------------------------------------

97. DATA PROTECTION CONTACT

For privacy-related questions, please use the official contact method provided with the Software or on the official project website.

Do not contact random people claiming to be developers.

Do not message the Captain's cousin.

Do not message the parrot.

The parrot does not check email.

------------------------------------------------------------

98. CHANGES IN OWNERSHIP

If the Software or project is transferred, merged, acquired, or otherwise becomes someone else's problem, applicable privacy practices may change.

Where required, users will be notified appropriately.

The Captain may sell the ship.

There is no ship.

We need to stop mentioning the ship.

------------------------------------------------------------

99. DATA PROCESSING PRINCIPLES

We aim to follow several general principles:

- Collect only what is reasonably necessary.
- Use information for legitimate purposes.
- Protect information appropriately.
- Avoid unnecessary retention.
- Provide transparency.
- Respect applicable privacy rights.

These are serious principles.

The parrot agrees.

The parrot has no idea what they mean.

------------------------------------------------------------

100. FINAL PRIVACY STATEMENT

Your privacy matters.

Your personal information matters.

Your passwords matter.

Your files matter.

Your computer matters.

Your questionable Downloads folder also matters.

We aim to treat information responsibly and transparently.

We do not want your passwords.

We do not want your bank information.

We do not want your private conversations.

We do not want your diary.

We do not want your embarrassing screenshots.

We do not want your browser history.

We especially do not want to know what you searched five minutes ago.

Please keep those things to yourself.

------------------------------------------------------------

101. THE FINAL FINAL PRIVACY STATEMENT

We have now reached the end of this Privacy Policy.

If you actually read all of it:

Congratulations.

You have demonstrated patience that the Developers do not possess.

If you skipped everything:

We understand.

If you scrolled directly to the bottom:

We expected that.

If you searched for the word \"password\":

We know.

JUST KIDDING.

We don't know.

That's the point.

------------------------------------------------------------

102. THE PARROT'S FINAL PRIVACY STATEMENT

The parrot would like to remind you:

DO NOT GIVE STRANGERS YOUR PASSWORD.

The parrot would also like a cracker.

The Developers cannot guarantee the cracker.

The parrot is disappointed.

------------------------------------------------------------

103. THE CAPTAIN'S FINAL PRIVACY STATEMENT

The Captain would like to confirm:

We do not need your password.

We do not need your credit card.

We do not need your home address.

We do not need your secrets.

We do not need your browser history.

We do not need your treasure map.

We do not need your pirate credentials.

We do not need your parrot.

Please keep the parrot.

------------------------------------------------------------

104. ABSOLUTELY FINAL DISCLAIMER

This Privacy Policy is intended to explain privacy practices in a general and understandable manner.

It is not legal advice.

It does not create a lawyer-client relationship.

The Developers are not acting as your attorneys.

The Captain is not an attorney.

The parrot is not an attorney.

The parrot has never attended law school.

The parrot cannot represent you in court.

Please stop asking the parrot.

------------------------------------------------------------

105. THANK YOU

Thank you for taking the time to read this Privacy Policy.

Your privacy is important.

Your security is important.

Your personal information is important.

Your computer is important.

Your sanity after reading this document is also important.

Please take a break.

Drink some water.

Look away from the screen.

Touch grass.

Return to the installer.

Click the button.

Install the questionable software.

ARRR.

------------------------------------------------------------

END OF PRIVACY POLICY

No personal information was harmed during the writing of this document.

The Captain remains anonymous.

The parrot remains loud.

Your Downloads folder remains suspicious.

Thank you."
