text = "BINDING POLICY
Because apparently Terms & Conditions weren't confusing enough.

Effective Date: Whenever we remember to update this page
Last Updated: Probably today
Document Version: 7.3.1.2.9-final-final-REAL

Welcome to the Binding Policy of [SKETCHY EMULATION APP NAME], hereafter referred to as \"The Program,\" \"The Software,\" \"This Thing,\" \"Our Magnificent Piece of Software,\" or, when it crashes, \"that stupid emulator.\"

By installing, opening, launching, clicking, double-clicking, accidentally launching, staring at, or otherwise acknowledging the existence of this application, you agree to the following Binding Policy.

If you do not agree, please close the installer.

If you cannot close the installer, please restart your computer.

If restarting your computer does not work, please reconsider your life choices.

1. WHAT IS THIS POLICY?

This Binding Policy exists to establish the extremely important rules governing the relationship between you, the Program, and the mysterious people who apparently thought making an emulator installer was a good idea.

Unlike the Terms and Conditions, which explain what you may or may not do, and the Privacy Policy, which explains what information we may or may not care about, this document exists primarily to make the word \"binding\" appear enough times that it sounds legally intimidating.

This policy is binding.

Very binding.

Extremely binding.

Like a zip file.

2. THE BINDING

By continuing past this screen, you acknowledge that this policy is binding upon you.

You are also bound by the Terms and Conditions.

You are bound by the Privacy Policy.

You are bound by the laws of wherever you happen to be.

You are bound by the laws of wherever the developer happens to be.

You are bound by common sense, although we understand that may be asking a lot.

You are not, however, physically bound to your chair.

We cannot do that.

Probably.

3. INSTALLATION BINDING

Installing the Program means you have voluntarily chosen to place our files somewhere on your computer.

We appreciate your confidence.

Once installed, the Program may create folders, files, shortcuts, configuration files, logs, caches, temporary files, extremely important-looking files, and other things that computers apparently require in order to function.

You agree not to become emotionally attached to any of these files.

You further agree that deleting random files from the installation directory and then complaining that the Program no longer works is not considered a valid warranty claim.

If you uninstall the Program and discover that you have deleted something important beforehand, that is between you and your Recycle Bin.

4. THE \"I DIDN'T READ IT\" CLAUSE

We know you didn't read this.

We know.

We saw you scroll directly to the bottom.

You probably didn't read the Terms and Conditions either.

You probably didn't read the Privacy Policy.

You may have even clicked \"I Agree\" without looking at what you were agreeing to.

This document therefore contains a special clause stating that you are still responsible for everything you agreed to, even if you didn't read it.

Nice try.

5. EMULATION BINDING

The Program may be used for legitimate emulation-related purposes.

What constitutes a legitimate purpose depends on applicable law and circumstances.

We provide software.

We do not provide legal advice.

We do not provide legal representation.

We are definitely not your lawyer.

Please stop emailing us asking whether something is legal.

We don't know.

Our legal department consists of a guy named Carlos who once watched a YouTube video about copyright law.

Carlos is not a lawyer.

Carlos has specifically requested that we mention this.

6. ROMS, ISOS, BIOS FILES, AND OTHER THINGS

The Program may allow you to interact with files commonly associated with emulation.

You are responsible for ensuring that you have the appropriate rights to possess, create, download, copy, modify, or use those files.

We do not magically grant you ownership of anything simply because our software can open it.

If you put something into the emulator and it works, congratulations.

If you put something into the emulator and it explodes your computer, congratulations on discovering experimental computing.

7. THE PIRATE CLAUSE

Despite the suspicious appearance of this application, the presence of pirate-themed graphics, and the fact that the developer may occasionally use words like \"ARRR,\" we do not claim that you are an actual pirate.

You may not use this software to board ships.

You may not demand treasure.

You may not make people walk the plank.

You may not replace your Windows login sound with an unnecessarily loud pirate scream and then blame us.

You may, however, wear a pirate hat while using the Program.

That is between you and the hat.

8. PERFORMANCE BINDING

The Program is provided on an \"it works on our computer\" basis.

Your computer may:

Run the Program perfectly.
Run the Program slowly.
Run the Program extremely slowly.
Run the Program while making concerning noises.
Refuse to run the Program.
Run the Program at 900 FPS for absolutely no reason.
Run the Program at 3 FPS despite having a graphics card capable of rendering the universe.
Crash because you have 47 Chrome tabs open.
Crash because you have 2 Chrome tabs open.
Crash because computers are complicated.

We are not responsible for your computer deciding that today is the day it becomes a toaster.

9. THE RAM CLAUSE

If the Program displays an error stating:

ERROR: NOT ENOUGH RAM

you are encouraged to remain calm.

Do not immediately purchase 128 GB of RAM.

Do not throw your computer out of a window.

Do not contact NASA.

Do not blame the refrigerator.

The error may be entirely fictional.

Or it may be entirely accurate.

We don't know.

10. UPDATES

The Program may receive updates.

Updates may:

Add features.
Remove features.
Change features.
Break features.
Fix things.
Accidentally break the things that were previously fixed.
Introduce completely unrelated problems.
Make the installer look slightly different.
Change absolutely nothing.

By installing an update, you acknowledge that software development is essentially controlled chaos with version numbers.

11. BETA SOFTWARE

If you are using a beta, experimental, development, preview, unstable, nightly, cursed, haunted, or otherwise suspicious version of the Program, additional binding applies.

You agree that:

Things may break.

Things may break spectacularly.

Your save file might break.

Your configuration might break.

The emulator might break.

The installer might break.

The developer's sanity might break.

You have been warned.

12. BUGS

If you find a bug, congratulations!

You have discovered free entertainment.

You may report the bug if a reporting system exists.

Please provide useful information such as:

What happened.
What you expected to happen.
What actually happened.
What you clicked.
What version you were using.
Whether you sacrificed a goat beforehand.

The last one is optional.

Please do not report:

\"IT DOESN'T WORK\"

with no additional information.

That tells us approximately as much as saying:

\"The computer is being computer.\"

13. FEATURE REQUESTS

You may request features.

You may politely request features.

You may enthusiastically request features.

You may not demand that we add your incredibly specific feature by tomorrow morning.

For example:

\"Please add support for this obscure console manufactured in 1998 by a company that disappeared in 2003.\"

is a reasonable request.

\"ADD IT OR I WILL FIND YOU\"

is not.

Please choose the first option.

14. THE BUTTON BINDING

Buttons are provided for your convenience.

If a button says \"Install,\" it probably installs something.

If a button says \"Cancel,\" it probably cancels something.

If a button says \"Continue,\" it probably continues something.

If a button says \"Do Not Press,\" you already pressed it, didn't you?

We know.

15. THE \"BACK\" BUTTON

The Back button is not a time machine.

Pressing Back will not undo:

Poor decisions.
Deleted files.
Embarrassing messages.
Your search history.
That thing you said in 2017.
Installing the wrong version of the emulator.

We apologize for any inconvenience.

16. THE INTERNET BINDING

Some parts of the Program may require an Internet connection.

Internet connections are mysterious.

They can disappear.

They can become slow.

They can decide that your computer is connected to the Internet but simultaneously refuse to connect to anything.

We are not responsible for your Internet Service Provider deciding that you have had enough Internet for today.

17. THIRD-PARTY SOFTWARE

The Program may interact with software, libraries, operating systems, drivers, runtimes, frameworks, APIs, or other components created by people who are not us.

Those people are responsible for their own software.

We are responsible for ours.

The computer is responsible for being confusing.

Everyone has responsibilities.

18. MODIFICATION BINDING

You may modify the Program where legally permitted and where the applicable license allows it.

If you modify the Program and it stops working, please do not send us an email saying:

\"Your emulator is broken.\"

There is a very good chance you broke it.

We respect your creativity.

We do not respect your ability to blame us for it.

19. NO MAGIC WARRANTY

We do not guarantee that the Program will:

Work forever.
Work tomorrow.
Work on your particular computer.
Work with your particular game.
Work after you modified it.
Work after Windows updates itself at 3:00 AM.
Work after you deleted system32.
Work after you installed 14 suspicious antivirus programs.
Work after you downloaded \"RAM_BOOSTER_FINAL_REAL.exe.\"

Especially the last one.

Please stop downloading things called that.

20. LIABILITY

To the maximum extent permitted by applicable law, we are not responsible for damages arising from your use or inability to use the Program.

This includes, where legally permissible:

Lost files.
Lost saves.
Lost progress.
Lost sleep.
Lost patience.
Lost dignity.
Lost friendships because you spent 14 hours configuring an emulator instead of going outside.

Please go outside occasionally.

21. COMMON SENSE

You agree to use common sense.

If something looks dangerous, suspicious, broken, radioactive, haunted, or like it was designed by someone who hasn't slept in three days, exercise caution.

The Program cannot physically stop you from doing stupid things.

Unfortunately.

22. ENFORCEMENT

We reserve the right to enforce this Binding Policy where appropriate and legally permissible.

However, we would strongly prefer not to.

We have other things to do.

Like fixing bugs.

And making the next version.

And wondering why someone is trying to emulate a game from a console that nobody has heard of.

23. CHANGES TO THIS POLICY

This Binding Policy may change.

When it does, the updated version will become the new Binding Policy.

If the policy changes significantly, we may inform you.

If it changes because we corrected a typo from \"emultor\" to \"emulator,\" we probably won't.

You are encouraged to occasionally check this document.

You will probably not.

That's okay.

We know.

24. SEVERABILITY

If one part of this policy is found to be invalid, unenforceable, ridiculous, poorly written, grammatically questionable, or somehow printed upside-down, the remaining sections shall remain effective to the extent permitted by applicable law.

Basically:

If one piece falls apart, we glue the rest back together.

Very professional.

25. ENTIRE AGREEMENT

This Binding Policy, together with the Terms and Conditions, Privacy Policy, applicable licenses, and any other documents explicitly incorporated into them, constitutes the entire agreement between you and the Program regarding the matters covered herein.

That is a very fancy way of saying:

\"Please read the other documents too.\"

26. FINAL BINDING STATEMENT

By clicking \"I Agree,\" \"Accept,\" \"Continue,\" \"Install,\" or any other button that reasonably indicates acceptance, you acknowledge that:

You have been given an opportunity to read this Binding Policy.

You understand that this document is intended to be binding to the extent permitted by applicable law.

You understand that we are not your lawyer.

You understand that Carlos is definitely not your lawyer.

You understand that emulation does not automatically give you the right to use copyrighted software.

You understand that your computer is not legally classified as a pirate ship.

You understand that downloading suspicious files is generally a bad idea.

You understand that pressing random buttons may produce random results.

You understand that this document is intentionally much longer than necessary.

And most importantly:

You understand that you have scrolled through approximately 4,000 words of legally questionable pirate nonsense just to reach this button.

Congratulations.

You may now continue.

BINDING POLICY ACCEPTED."
